00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.900
So I am the Web Accessibility Specialist
here at Stanford.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.440
I work out of the University of
Communications.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.970
I work with Scott Stocker, for any folks
who may know who he is.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:28.106
He's been at Stanford for a number of
years, so lots

00:00:28.106 --> 00:00:32.255
of folks know who he is which helps me in
my work.

00:00:32.255 --> 00:00:37.150
And what my work consists of is a couple
of different things.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:42.460
One thing is that I assess Stanford
websites, particularly

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.190
focusing on high traffic sites and high
visibility sites.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.164
Assess them against the web content
accessibility guidelines.

00:00:51.164 --> 00:00:56.260
Stanford focuses on it, it's called WCAG
for short.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.720
You may hear people pronounce it
differently,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.540
but WCAG, mostly people know what you
mean.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.890
WCAG 2.0, and we focus on the Double A
Criteria.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:12.270
I'm not gonna launch into any discussion
of those criteria, what they mean.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:19.491
But, the short version to think about when
you think about WCAG is pour, P-O-U-R.

00:01:19.491 --> 00:01:27.596
Perceivable, perceivable,Operable, ew.

00:01:27.596 --> 00:01:28.576
&gt;&gt; Understandable.

00:01:28.576 --> 00:01:33.417
&gt;&gt; Thank you [LAUGH] Understandable and
Robust.

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:36.485
Was that my colleague there, JB, in the
back?

00:01:36.485 --> 00:01:37.252
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hi.

00:01:37.252 --> 00:01:39.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:43.490
&gt;&gt; So anyway, so those are the four, four
criteria.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.145
I don't actually think about them that way
very

00:01:46.145 --> 00:01:48.490
often, cuz I kinda know em back and forth.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.153
So that's, that's why I'm a little
[INAUDIBLE].

