01:00:42.780 --> 01:00:47.930
But before that my last principle, this is
a little bit of love.

01:00:47.930 --> 01:00:50.270
For the sides to be compelling and

01:00:50.270 --> 01:00:54.520
for them to create delightful experience
to users,

01:00:54.520 --> 01:00:56.340
we have to meet and exceed users

01:00:56.340 --> 01:00:59.520
expectations, and the same is true for
accessibility.

01:00:59.520 --> 01:01:04.780
In addition to focusing on headings,
keyboard navigation, and non text content.

01:01:04.780 --> 01:01:10.320
We also have to augment out HTML document
with some additional attributes, that

01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:15.590
convey this extra information to screen
leaders, and that is done with ARIA.

01:01:15.590 --> 01:01:18.810
ARIA stands for Accessible Rich Internet
Applications.

01:01:18.810 --> 01:01:21.160
And it's a set of rules, states, and
properties that you

01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:25.810
can associate with an HTML element to talk
to the screen reader.

01:01:25.810 --> 01:01:26.310
So,

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:31.430
for, in order to make this fake button
accessible,

01:01:31.430 --> 01:01:35.220
I would actually give it an ARIA rule of
button.

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:40.050
This tells the screen reader, that this
element is actually a button.

01:01:40.050 --> 01:01:43.190
To make it get that focus when you talk to
it,

01:01:43.190 --> 01:01:45.960
I need to add a tab index of zero to it,

01:01:45.960 --> 01:01:48.330
and I also need to make sure that it
interacts to

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:52.350
key, with keyboard errands, just like it
does with mouse errands.

